//! @zpm/va nvenc — the RTX's video encoder as this driver programs it:
//! the class, its methods, where the fields of its picture setup are, the
//! status it writes, the input picture's block-linear copy, and one
//! picture's pushbuffer.
//!
//! What is public stops at Ada: the methods are NVC9B7's (open-gpu-doc
//! classes/video/clc9b7.h; Turing's NVC5B7 and Ampere's NVC7B7 put them at
//! the same offsets) and the structs are nvenc_drv.h's. NVIDIA's open
//! kernel module names GB20x's encoder class NVCFB7_VIDEO_ENCODER (0xcfb7)
//! beside NVC9B7 in msencGetEngineDescFromAllocParams, but publishes no
//! methods or structs for it: Blackwell's are ASSUMED to be Ada's (THE
//! RISK, as for the decoder). The firmware checks the setup's magic word
//! first and stops with ENC_ERROR_BAD_MAGIC (the status says so) when the
//! layout is not the one it knows; GALAXY_VA_NVENC_MAGIC sets another.
//!
//! No open driver programs NVENC directly (NVIDIA's own is closed; Tegra's
//! user space is closed; nouveau/NVK has no encoder): everything here is
//! from the two headers. The field positions below were generated by gcc
//! from nvenc_drv.h (each field set to all ones in a zeroed struct, its
//! bits found): bit fields of mixed types are laid out as the firmware's
//! compiler lays them out, which hand-made packed structs would risk
//! getting wrong.
//!
//! What the engine does and what the driver does (nvenc_drv.h):
//! - the engine writes each slice: start code, NAL unit header, slice
//!   header (unless disable_slice_header) and the slice data, from
//!   bitstream_start_pos on in the output buffer;
//! - the driver writes everything above the slice layer: H.264's SPS and
//!   PPS (and SEI), HEVC's VPS, SPS and PPS (enc_h264.sig, enc_hevc.sig).
//!   The setup's sps_data/pps_data only tell the engine what those
//!   parameter sets said, for the slice headers it writes.
//! - reference and reconstructed pictures are the engine's own "tiled
//!   16x16" layout (it writes them, it reads them: the CPU never does);
//! - the input picture is block linear (GPU BL, `input_bl_mode` 0) or the
//!   engine's tiled 16x16: nvenc_drv.h 1.5 (2) says input pictures are
//!   "either all in BL format or all in tiled 16x16 format", and its
//!   surface configuration has no pitch-linear mode (block_height,
//!   tiled_16x16, memory_mode semi-planar/planar, nv21, input_bl_mode GPU,
//!   Tegra or the C model's "naive" BL: nothing else). A block-linear
//!   surface is read where it is (any block height: `input_cfg_block_height`
//!   is log2 of GOBs per block, as NVIDIA's DRM modifier says it); a
//!   pitch-linear picture needs a pass into block linear first (the
//!   driver's converter, the GPU's in the library; `tileNv12` is the CPU's
//!   pass, kept for tests).
//! - rate control: SET_CONTROL_PARAMS' RCMODE (nvenc_drv.h 3.1: 0 constant
//!   QP, 1 picture-level VBR, 2 picture-level CBR, 3 VBR with a minimum
//!   QP, 17..19 the same per macroblock row, 32 activity based) with the
//!   setup's rate_control struct and the persistent state in the RC process
//!   buffer (nvenc_persistent_state_s). Its fixed-point fields' units
//!   (Np, Bmin, Ravg, R) are not documented: enc_rc.sig says what is filled
//!   from them and when the firmware's control is used at all.
//!
//! AV1 (NVC9B7's AV1, nvenc_av1_drv_pic_setup_s): the same methods plus
//! the probability (CDF) buffers in and out, and the entropy, deblocking,
//! CDEF and loop-restoration line buffers (0x480..0x49c); the status
//! (nvenc_av1_pic_stat_s) reports the QP and loop-filter level "to be
//! inserted into frame header by driver" (enc_av1.sig writes the headers).
//!
//! Pure: no calls, no state.

const std = @import("std");
const tiling = @import("tiling.sig");
const nvdec = @import("nvdec.sig");

// ── The class and its methods (clc9b7.h) ───────────────────────────────────

/// NVCFB7_VIDEO_ENCODER: GB20x's encoder class as NVIDIA's open kernel
/// module lists it (clcfb7.h); its methods ASSUMED NVC9B7's.
pub const CLASS: u32 = 0xcfb7;
/// SET_OBJECT's ENGINE field, as the decoder's (nvdec.sig).
pub const SET_OBJECT_ENGINE_SW: u32 = nvdec.SET_OBJECT_ENGINE_SW;

pub const SET_OBJECT: u32 = 0x000;
